CAT = "cisPackage"; SEG = "PKG"
Q = [
("What is the main purpose of sterilization packaging?",
 "Let sterilant in, keep microbes out", ["Make sets lighter to carry", "Show the set's catalog number", "Dry instruments after cleaning"],
 "Packaging must allow the sterilant to reach the contents and then act as a sterile barrier until the package is opened at the point of use."),
("According to CDC, what clearance should sterilization packaging materials have?",
 "FDA 510(k) clearance", ["A state health license", "An OSHA permit", "An EPA registration"],
 "CDC recommends that packaging materials be compatible with the sterilization process and have received FDA 510(k) clearance."),
("CDC says sterilization packaging should be strong enough to do what?",
 "Resist punctures and tears", ["Stand upright on a shelf", "Be reused at least once", "Hold water for an hour"],
 "CDC recommends packaging sufficiently strong to resist punctures and tears so the sterile barrier holds during storage and handling."),
("Which is a disposable, nonwoven sterilization wrap?",
 "SMS polypropylene wrap", ["Muslin cotton wrap", "Anodized aluminum case", "Reusable woven barrier"],
 "Spunbond-meltblown-spunbond (SMS) polypropylene is a common single-use nonwoven wrap. Woven textile wraps are laundered and reused."),
("Why must reusable woven wraps be tracked and inspected after laundering?",
 "Washing wears the barrier down", ["They change color in steam", "They grow heavier each use", "They block chemical indicators"],
 "Each laundering can thin fibers or cause holes. Woven wraps are checked on a light table and retired according to the maker's instructions."),
("Which package type lets the contents be seen and is often used for single instruments?",
 "Paper-plastic peel pouch", ["Rigid aluminum container", "Double SMS sheet wrap", "Woven textile wrap"],
 "Peel pouches have a clear plastic film and a paper side through which the sterilant passes. They suit light, single items."),
("A reusable woven sterilization wrap is usually made of which material?",
 "Cotton-polyester fabric", ["Spunbond polypropylene", "Tyvek film", "Medical kraft paper"],
 "Woven wraps are textiles, often a cotton and polyester blend, laundered between uses. Polypropylene SMS is a disposable nonwoven, and Tyvek is used in low-temperature pouches."),
("What must be inspected on a rigid sterilization container before each use?",
 "Gaskets, filters and latches", ["The color of the outer label", "The age of the instruments", "The weight of the lid only"],
 "Container seals depend on intact gaskets, filters or valves and closing latches. A damaged gasket breaks the sterile barrier."),
("Which factor helps decide between a wrap and a rigid container for a set?",
 "The sterilization method", ["The surgeon's preferred color", "The day of the week", "The number of staff on shift"],
 "The outline lists technique, gaskets and weight limits as factors. The container or wrap must be validated for the sterilizer and cycle used."),
("Why must a rigid container and its contents stay within the maker's weight limit?",
 "Heavy loads can stay wet", ["Heavy sets cannot be counted", "Heavy sets need no indicator", "Heavy sets cost more to wrap"],
 "Excess metal mass holds condensate, which can lead to wet packs and a compromised barrier. The container's IFU gives the weight it was validated for."),
("A heat sealer for pouches should be checked routinely for what?",
 "A complete, even seal", ["The color of the pouch", "The time of the last case", "The weight of the instrument"],
 "The outline lists routine maintenance and quality testing of heat sealers. Seal checks confirm the temperature and pressure give a seal with no channels or gaps."),
("A peel pouch seal shows a wrinkle that runs across the seal width. What should be done?",
 "Repackage the item", ["Tape over the wrinkle", "Use it if the indicator changed", "Sterilize it a second time"],
 "A channel through the seal is a path for microbes, so the sterile barrier is not intact. The item is repackaged in a new pouch."),
("Why is an internal chemical indicator placed inside a package as well as an external one outside?",
 "To show sterilant reached inside", ["To replace the biological indicator", "To mark the set as an implant", "To count the instruments"],
 "The external indicator only shows the package was exposed to the process. The internal indicator shows conditions inside where the sterilant had to reach."),
("A set is wrapped with two sheets folded one over the other at the same time. What is this method called?",
 "Simultaneous wrapping", ["Sequential wrapping", "Envelope pouching", "Single-sheet wrapping"],
 "Simultaneous wrapping uses two bonded or stacked sheets folded as one. Sequential wrapping folds one sheet, then a second sheet over it."),
("Why are anodized aluminum container lids and bottoms often color coded?",
 "To identify the set", ["To show the set is sterile", "To mark the expiry date", "To show the weight limit"],
 "Colored anodizing lets staff see at a glance which department or set a container belongs to. Sterility is shown by indicators, not by color."),
("A set is to be sterilized by a low-temperature method. What must be true of its packaging and contents?",
 "Both are labeled for that method", ["All items fit in one tray", "Every item is the same metal", "The set is under warranty"],
 "Some materials melt, absorb or block a sterilant. Both the packaging and the instruments must be compatible with, and labeled for, the method chosen."),
("Why should a peel pouch not be filled so tightly that it bulges?",
 "The seal can burst or fail", ["The pouch will not fit a rack", "The label cannot be read", "The indicator turns too early"],
 "An overfilled pouch puts stress on the seals, which can open during the cycle or handling and break the sterile barrier. A larger pouch is used instead."),
("A heavy orthopedic set will be wrapped. What helps the sterile barrier survive handling?",
 "A smooth tray and corner guards", ["A single thin wrap", "Tape wound around the whole set", "The count sheet taped outside"],
 "Corners and protruding parts puncture wraps. Smooth trays and protectors keep the wrap intact, which is the purpose of packaging."),
("What should a technician check on a rigid container's filter retention plate?",
 "It is seated and locked", ["It is painted the right color", "It is lighter than the lid", "It has a new date sticker"],
 "If the plate holding the filter is loose, the filter can shift and leave an open path into the container."),
("A peel pouch with a heavy instrument has a pinhole in the plastic after sterilization. What is its status?",
 "Contaminated and repackaged", ["Sterile if the indicator changed", "Usable for minor cases", "Sterile if used today"],
 "Any breach of the barrier means the contents are no longer sterile. Heavy or pointed items in pouches should be protected to prevent holes."),
]
